From a69e69a0fe35a4efa052885f263e08a0494cfd0a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Project Luma <builds@project-luma.local>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 19:01:11 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] luma: Beam, the volume and brightness level indicator

GNOME's on-screen display is a centred pill 4em above the bottom of the
monitor, unrelated to the shelf. Luma presents it as Beam: a 236 x 48 capsule
whose body fills from the leading edge to the level, with the glyph at the
leading end and the value at the trailing end. It uses the Quick Options
surface of the current appearance mode (fill, 1px line, both shadows, and the
compositor blur on Frost and Glass); the fill is the active control colour at
24% ending in a 2px marker at 55%. Above 100% the fill stops at full width
and the number shows the real value.

Beam is a presentation of OsdWindow, not a new service: OsdWindowManager and
show/showOne/showAll keep their API, so the volume indicator, brightness,
gnome-settings-daemon's ShowOSD and Mutter's show-osd reach it unchanged. The
kind of request is read from the icon every caller sends. Requests without a
level show the icon and the caller's short label.

It is placed by the shelf surface rule against the island holding Quick
Options, and where no shelf is shown (fullscreen, the lock screen, a monitor
without the shelf) it is centred on the monitor, 8px inside the work-area
edge the shelf uses. It updates in place, hides 1500ms after the last change,
fades in over 100ms while moving 6px away from the shelf, and animates its
fill over 100ms; with reduced motion there is no movement and no fill
animation. It never takes focus or pointer input, never shows while Quick
Options is open, and opening Quick Options dismisses it. Screen readers hear
"Volume 48 percent", "Volume muted" or "Brightness 72 percent" through a
polite ATK notification.

The volume and brightness glyphs are drawn from Lucide geometry (ISC).

Dropping this patch restores GNOME's on-screen display.
